		<!--BEGIN MAIN BODY CONTENT--> <table width=3D"443" cellspacing=3D"0" cel=
lpadding=3D"0" border=3D"0"> <tbody><tr> <td width=3D"443"> <!--BEGIN CONTE=
NT--> <!-- ******** PASTE MAIN BODY CONTENT HERE ******** --> <table cellsp=
acing=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" border=3D"0"> <tbody><tr valign=3D"top"><td=
 width=3D"438"> <table width=3D"443" cellspacing=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" bo=
rder=3D"0"> <tbody><tr> <td width=3D"443" valign=3D"top" height=3D"19" clas=
s=3D"v14-header-1efix"><b>&nbsp;Technote (FAQ)</b></td></tr> <tr><td>&nbsp;=
</td></tr> <tr> <td width=3D"443" valign=3D"bottom" height=3D"20"><b>Questi=
on</b></td></tr> <tr> <td width=3D"443"> How do I set up and use HSM&#x27;s=
 PDA trace facility?  </td></tr> <tr><td>&nbsp;</td></tr> </tbody></table><=
/td></tr> <tr valign=3D"top"><td width=3D"438"></td></tr><tr> <td width=3D"=
443" valign=3D"bottom" height=3D"20"><b>Cause</b></td></tr> <tr> <td width=
=3D"443"> Customer is not using the HSM PDA and wishes to do so. </td></tr>=
 <tr><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>  <tr valign=3D"top"><td width=3D"438"></td></tr><=
tr><td width=3D"443" valign=3D"bottom" height=3D"20"><b>Answer</b></td></tr=
> <tr> <td width=3D"443"> The HSM PDA trace function is designed to facilit=
ate first-failure data capture of problems that may occur during HSM proces=
sing.  The trace facility requires minimal system resources with negligible=
 performance degredation to HSM.
<p>To enable the HSM PDA, you must first ensure the PDOX and PDOY datasets=
 are preallocated.  PDA datasets can be SMS managed or non-SMS managed. If=
 you decide to SMS-manage them, you must use GUARANTEED SPACE and it is rec=
ommended that you assign them to a management class that does not perform=
 MIGRATION or BACKUP.
</p><p>Here is an example of an allocation for non-SMS managed PDA datasets=
:<br> </p><pre>    =20
//ARCPDOX  DD DSN=3D?UID.HSMPDOX,DISP=3D(,CATLG),VOL=3DSER=3D?TRACEVOL,  =
               =20
//         UNIT=3D?TRACEUNIT,SPACE=3D(CYL,(20,2))                        =
           =20
//ARCPDOY  DD DSN=3D?UID.HSMPDOY,DISP=3D(,CATLG),VOL=3DSER=3D?TRACEVOL,  =
              =20
//         UNIT=3D?TRACEUNIT,SPACE=3D(CYL,(20,2))       =20
</pre><br>
Both datasets must be allocated on the same volume.  The size of the datase=
ts is dependent on how busy your system is and how much trace info you want=
 the PDA to contain.  You can tune the size of the PDA datasets by monitori=
ng for ARC0037I messages while PDA is enabled.  HSM generates an ARC0037I=
 message each time the PDA datasets swap.  Tune the size of the PDOX and PD=
OY datasets until the PDA datasets swap at an acceptable frequency.  A typi=
cal nominal value for this is between one and three hours.<br> <br>
Once the PDA datasets are allocated, it must be enabled.  There are three=
 places where the PDA dataset can be controlled:<br> <br>
   1.  In the HSM STARTUP PROC, ensure that &#x27;PDA=3DYES&#x27;.  This wi=
ll ensure that PDA tracing begins at startup (i.e., during the HSM startup=
 procedure.)<br> <br>
   2.  In the HSM ARCCMDxx PARMLIB member, ensure the command &#x27;SETSYS=
 PDA(ON)&#x27; is present..<br> <br>
   3. While HSM is processing, the PDA can be toggled ON/OFF interactively=
 with the commands:<br> <br>
        HSEND SETSYS PDA(ON)<br>
        HSEND SETSYS PDA(OFF)<br> <br>
When  PDA is enabled, you can manually swap the PDA datasets with the comma=
nd :<br> <br>
       HSEND SWAPLOG PDA<br> <br>
The HSM PDA holds trace information for at least as much time as the capaci=
ty of one PDA dataset up to the amount the two datasets can hold combined=
 (depending on how long it has been since the last swap.)  You can set up=
 a system of retaining PDA trace info for days, weeks, or months by using=
 an auto-op program and a GDG.   You can do this by:<br> <br>
     1  Set up an auto-op job (such as in NetView) to key on the ARC0037I=
 message that is generated when the PDOX and PDOY datasets are swapped.<br>=
 <br>
     2.  Allocate a GDG to copy the contents of the PDOY dataset into.<br>=
 <br>
     3.  Create a job  that the auto-op job will start that copies the PDOY=
 dataset into the GDG that you allocated in step 2.  <br> <br>
For this technique to work, you must ensure that your PDA datasets are larg=
e enough so they will not swap while the auto-op job is copying the PDOY da=
taset from the previous swap.  PDA processing will fail if it is unable to=
 enqueue on the PDOY dataset to swap the PDA logs.<br> <br> <br>
Once you have PDA enabled, you can determine the time period that a particu=
lar PDA dataset covers by usine BROWSE in ISPF with the dataset.  The first=
 record in the PDA dataset is a timestamp that indicates when the first rec=
ord was written.  It will look like this:<br> <br>
11049.TIME....=C3=B7=C3=9C.=C3=9A045718.G=C3=9F=C2=A8N2  ..=C2=AF1H*MCVTFPD=
A*X...6 ....... <br> <br>
This PDA timestamp was made on the 049 Julian date of 2011 at 04:57:18.<br>=
 <br>
HSM writes these timestamps periodically through the PDA.  To find the last=
 timestamp, page to the bottom of the dataset and do a reverse find for &#x=
27;TIME&#x27;.<br> <br> <br> <br>  </td></tr> <tr><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>  <tr=
 valign=3D"top"><td width=3D"438"></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr> <tr=
